eu education ministers will look at ways  to ensure that today's young people are  equipped with the necessary skills to  succeed in an increasingly digital  environment they will also review the  achievements of european cooperation in  the fields of education and training and  assess whether there is room to improve  this cooperation in the future  following up on the informal meeting of  eu education ministers in march in paris  the council will also stress the  importance of promoting citizenship and  the common values of freedom tolerance  and non-discrimination through education  aware that youth unemployment rates  remain high and so does the risk of  social exclusion the council will also  consider how to reinforce youth work and  how to better address the socio-economic  challenges facing young people  the council will also discuss the future  european audiovisual policy the  convergence of online and traditional  media is becoming a reality for more and  more european citizens it opens up new  opportunities for industry and consumers  but it also brings new challenges for  policy makers and regulators including  how to ensure the quality and the  cultural diversity of the digital offer  as concerns cultural heritage issues  ministers will also be briefed about the  extent of the destruction of cultural  sites perpetrated by isis daesh in iraq  and syria finally eu ministers will seek  to maximize the role of grassroots sport  in the life of citizens especially young  people to enhance their participation in  society and their employability
 

